A recurring headache for the web site owner and user is the humble PDF. Ubiquitous, essential, and the closest we've come to an immutable file format -- I can more easily open a PDF [from 30 years ago](https://web.archive.org/web/20051103044315/http://www.pdf-tools.com/public/downloads/pdf-reference/pdfreference12.pdf) than a web page from 20 years back (anyone else remember Flash?). On PreventionWeb.net, where research, reports, and guidance documents are central to the user experience, the way we present PDFs matters. Yet we often leave users with a choice: do you want the web page with added context and related materials or the PDF download. Better to be able to have both.

## Why is this still a problem?

You'd think, in 2025, that there'd be a drop-in, lightweight, accessible PDF viewer that just works. But after poking around, most solutions are either heavy, locked behind paywalls, or just not quite right for our needs. I want something that:

- Loads quickly
- Easy UX metaphor for mobile and desktop
- Is accessible (keyboard navigation, ARIA, screen reader support)
- Lets us customize the UI (show/hide controls, tweak the look)
- Supports deep linking and shareable page URLs
- Some search support
- Doesn't drag in a pile of dependencies

## Enter PDF-A-go-go

While there are 3rd party tools for this, such as embedding the raw PDF viewer or commercial services: these are either clunky, inconsistent, inflexible or just not great.

So I made a somewhat off-the-cuff named PDF-A-go-go: a small, embeddable PDF viewer. It's built on top of Mozilla's excellent [PDF.js](https://mozilla.github.io/pdf.js/), but strips things down to the essentials and adds a few features that make it a better fit for sites like PreventionWeb:

- **Side-scroll viewing**: Feels more like flipping through a magazine than a clunky old document viewer.
- **Customizable controls**: Show or hide search, page selectors, download buttons, and more—just by tweaking data attributes.
- **Accessible by default**: Keyboard navigation, ARIA labels, and screen reader support are all baked in.
- **Shareable links**: Every page in a PDF can be linked to directly, making it easier to reference specific sections in reports or guidance docs.
- **Mobile friendly**: Works well on phones and tablets, not just desktops.

Integration is as simple as dropping in a JS and CSS file, then adding a container div with a few data attributes. No React, no build step, no fuss. Here's what it looks like in practice:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://khawkins98.github.io/PDF-A-go-go/pdf-a-go-go.css">
<script src="https://khawkins98.github.io/PDF-A-go-go/pdf-a-go-go.js"></script>
<div class="pdfagogo-container"
     data-pdf-url="./example.pdf"
     data-show-search="true"
     data-show-prev-next="true"
     data-show-page-selector="true"
     data-show-current-page="true"
     data-show-download="true"></div>
```

Want to hide the download button? Just set `data-show-download="false"`. Need to start on page 4? Add `data-default-page="4"`. It's all in the markup.

Here's a live example of PDF-A-go-go in action. For more info or to contribute, check out the project on <a href="https://github.com/khawkins98/PDF-A-go-go" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.

